{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"REVISED after review round 1 (7 blocks). Three were genuine holes in my design, and one was a bug in\nmy own acceptance criterion. All verified in source before acting.\n\n1. PAGINATION IS A REMOTE PROPERTY, NOT A STORE-COPY PROPERTY. The sharpest finding. I attributed\n   pagination exercise to 'realistic volume' from the copied store. Wrong: paging is driven by how\n   many issues exist IN JIRA. The store copy buys realistic local volume, ticket shapes and link\n   graphs — it does not exercise paging at all. The suite must CREATE issues in the harness. And\n   rather than assume a page size, the plan now READS the instance's effective cap first (Jira 8.17.1\n   defaults to 50 but jira.search.views.default.max is server-configurable and truncation is SILENT —\n   get_parent_map had exactly this defect), then seeds 2*observed+1 so three pages are required, and\n   reports the observed cap so a future instance change is visible.\n\n2. MY sync.remote ASSERTION WAS A BUG. I wrote that a non-empty sync.remote should fail the job. It\n   defaults to 'origin' (_config_schema.py:307) whether or not such a remote exists, so that check\n   would fail unconditionally — and 'fixing' it by setting sync.remote='' would give false confidence\n   while changing nothing. The assertion with real weight is that 'git remote' returns NOTHING. A\n   safety check that cannot pass is worse than none: someone will weaken it.\n\n3. THE STORE COPY MECHANISM WAS HAND-WAVING. '.tickets-tracker/' is GITIGNORED (.gitignore:9) and the\n   store lives on the ORPHAN 'tickets' branch, which actions/checkout does not fetch. 'Copies the\n   ticket-store content in' would have copied NOTHING. The job must explicitly fetch origin tickets\n   and materialise that tree.\n\n4. SEQUENCE A THIN SLICE FIRST. Fair process finding: the riskiest unknown is not any single mutation\n   but whether the copied-and-scrubbed store drives a live pass AT ALL. Landing thirteen mutations\n   against an unproven path risks debugging thirteen failures with one root cause. The plan now\n   requires fetch + scrub + isolation + ONE inbound and ONE outbound round-trip green in CI before the\n   full suite.\n\nRe-review running."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785511726956418001,"uuid":"c512e352-71ff-4409-855c-d0ccc4de964a"}